      "publication_date": "2021-12-01",
      "abstract": "Abstract While Lenin considered human knowledge to be similar to a mirror-like reflection of the object, Aleksandr Bogdanov emphasized the creative role of the subject in organizing the world. On the basis of some textual evidence, it is possible to describe the epistemologies of the two most influential Russian Marxists at the beginning of the twentieth century using the two metaphors of photography, on the one hand, and cinema, on the other. In particular, while discussing Einstein\u2019s relativity, Bogdanov considers sense organs, memory, and all the apparatus of human knowledge \u2018as a certain kind of cinematographic device\u2019. Sergei Eisenstein deems that cinema is \u2018an excellent instrument of perception \u2026 for the sensation of movement\u2019. Although it is difficult to find compelling proof of exchange and influence, this is an actual \u2018tangential point\u2019 between Bogdanov\u2019s and Eisenstein\u2019s ideas on human knowledge.",
